# Context
- Personal learning project: GTM analytics on Snowflake (synthetic data, dbt, semantic view, Cortex Analyst, managed MCP).
- The owner is learning Snowflake. Treat him as a senior analytics engineer who is new to Snowflake specifically.
- Everything is synthetic. Never add real company names, real metrics, or code from any employer.

# How to work
- Investigate read-only first. Before proposing a fix, read the relevant files and explain the cause.
- Business rules, metric definitions and model grain are decided by the human. Ask; do not invent them.
- Learning code is written by the human first: generator business logic in `generator/gtm_gen/entities.py`, stage and COPY INTO SQL in `infra/02_stage_and_load.sql`, dbt models, semantic view metrics. Review it like a senior reviewer instead of rewriting it, unless explicitly asked.
- Boilerplate (configs, CI, writers, CLI wiring) can be written directly.
- CI is the gate. Do not bypass or weaken checks to make them pass.

# Snowflake conventions
- Never quote identifiers. Write unquoted names; Snowflake stores them in uppercase.
- Default to XSMALL warehouses with AUTO_SUSPEND = 60. Any larger size must be temporary and justified.
- Never run DDL, DML or grants against Snowflake without showing the statement and getting approval first.
- Use the least privileged role for the task: LOADER for loading, TRANSFORMER for dbt, REPORTER or MCP_READONLY for reads. ACCOUNTADMIN only for account setup scripts in `infra/`.
- Never print, log or commit credentials, account identifiers or private keys.

# Writing rules for published text (README, docs/, any .md)
- Never use em dashes, en dashes, or a hyphen with spaces around it as punctuation. Use commas, periods, colons or parentheses.
- Do not describe this as professional Snowflake experience. It is a project.
- `scripts/check_dashes.py` enforces the dash rule in CI.
